@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-config Configuration
@brief The macros that enable and configure the framework.

@details
The framework is configured with preprocessor macros. They have no value;
only their definition matters:
- `MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED` is required; without it, the
  headers declare nothing and the library sources compile to nothing;
- `MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_DEPRECATED_ENABLED` makes the deprecated
  definitions available;
- `MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_TRACE_ENABLED` and
  `MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_TRACE_CONSTRUCTORS_ENABLED` enable the
  trace messages.

@par Where to define them
The application defines the macros; neither the library nor its CMake,
meson, and xPack build files define them (the `defaultDefine` entries in
`xcdl-package.jsonc` are informative only).

The application and the library sources must be compiled with the same
`MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED` and
`MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_DEPRECATED_ENABLED` macros, otherwise the
link fails (see the pages of the macros below); the trace macros only add
trace calls to the function bodies. The simplest way is a header named
`micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus-defines.h` (or
`micro-os-plus/project-config.h`) in the include path of the build: every
µTest++ header includes it, if present, before its own definitions.

Guard each definition, so that the macro can also be given on the compiler
command line:

@code{.cpp}
#if !defined(MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED)
#define MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED
#endif // !defined(MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED)
@endcode

A macro given only on the compiler command line (for example
`-DMICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED`) must be given for the whole
build, for both the application and the library sources.

The definitions shown on the pages of the macros, with a line number in
`micro-test-plus.h`, exist only for this documentation; the header does not
define these macros, and neither does the library.
